 25O  c  2016Ko11: C({+26}F,{+25}O), {+26}F beam was produced by fragmentation  
 25O 2c  of {+48}Ca primary                                                     
 25O 3c  beam, E=345 MeV/nucleon, on a Be target at RIKEN Nishina Center and    
 25O 4c  Center for Nuclear Study, University of Tokyo. The fragments were      
 25O 5c  separated by BigRIPS separator and {+26}F secondary beam bombarded     
 25O 6c  a C (thickness 1.8 g/cm{+2}) target with mean energies of              
 25O 7c  201 MeV/nucleon (mid target). The impact position on the target and    
 25O 8c  incident angles of beam particles were determined event by event using 
 25O 9c  two multiwire drift chambers (MWDCs). The decay products, {+24}O and   
 25O ac  neutron were measured in coincidence using a magnetic spectrometer,    
 25O bc  SAMURAI, for momentum analysis of charged particles and by             
 25O cc  plastic scintillator array, NEBULA, consisting of 120 detector modules 
 25O dc  and 24 charged particle veto detectors, respectively. The trajectories 
 25O ec  of the charged fragments were determined using two MWDCs and a 16      
 25O fc  element plastic scintillator hodoscope was used for energy loss        
 25O gc  and time-of-flight measurements.                                       
 25O  c  2013Ca18: C({+26}F,{+25}O), {+26}F beam, E=442 MeV/nucleon, produced   
 25O 2c  via fragmentation of a 490 MeV/nucleon {+40}Ar primary beam on a       
 25O 3c  4 g/cm{+2} {+9}Be                                                      
 25O 4c  target using the R3B-LAND reaction setup at GSI. Neutron-unbound       
 25O 5c  states in {+25}O were populated via one-proton knockout reactions on   
 25O 6c  different secondary targets: 922 mg/cm{+2} CH{-2}, 935 mg/cm{+2} C,    
 25O 7c  and 2145 mg/cm{+2} Pb. The secondary target was surrounded by the 4|p  
 25O 8c  Crystal Ball detector comprising 160 NaI(Tl) elements for photon and   
 25O 9c  light-particle detection. Energy-loss and position measurements for    
 25O ac  charged beam particles were accomplished using two silicon-strip       
 25O bc  detectors placed behind the target. Ions were identified through       
 25O cc  time-of-flight and energy-loss measurements. Neutrons were detected    
 25O dc  12 m downstream of the target using the LAND neutron detector with 92% 
 25O ec  single-neutron efficiency.                                             
 25O  c  2008Ho03: {+9}Be({+26}F,{+25}O), E=85 MeV/nucleon, {+26}F beam was     
 25O 2c  produced from fragmentation of {+48}Ca                                 
 25O 3c  beam on a Be target and using A1900 fragment separator at NSCL         
 25O 4c  facility. The {+26}F beam was counted on an event-by-event basis by    
 25O 5c  time-of-flight method. The neutrons emitted by {+25}O decay were       
 25O 6c  detected with a Modular Neutron array. The {+24}O fragments were       
 25O 7c  deflected by the large-gap Sweeper magnet, and were recorded           
 25O 8c  by position and energy sensitive detectors. Neutron peak from          
 25O 9c  {+25}O decay was observed at 770 keV.                                  
